course: everyday
lesson: 99
title: Mistakes, asking for help and saying no
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Everyone makes mistakes; owning up straight away, saying sorry and learning from them is respected far more than hiding them or blaming someone else, and you learn more by trying than by not trying.
explain: Asking for help is a strength: teachers want you to ask, and if you need to talk to someone privately there are free children's helplines with trained people who listen in confidence.
explain: Saying no is a skill too: to danger, to touches, hugs or kisses you do not want, to strangers online who ask for photos or information, and to cigarettes, vapes and alcohol, which harm a growing body.
explain: A boy in Leeds is dared to jump off the garage roof; he says «No, I'm not doing that» and walks away, and later his friends admit they were glad he did.
fact: If you break something by accident, tell an adult straight away and say sorry.
fact: You learn far more by trying than by not trying.
fact: One bad mark does not decide who you are; look at what you missed and ask for help.
fact: Blaming others for your mistake is unfair, and people stop trusting you.
fact: When someone else makes a mistake, be kind and do not laugh.
fact: Teachers want to help, and other pupils probably have the same question.
fact: Children's helplines are free and private: Childline 0800 1111 in the UK, Childline 1800 66 66 66 in Ireland, Kids Help Phone 1-800-668-6868 in Canada, Kids Helpline 1800 55 1800 in Australia, Youthline 0800 376 633 in New Zealand, and 988 in the USA.
fact: Your safety matters more than looking brave, so say no to dangerous dares.
fact: Your body belongs to you; you can say no to a hug or touch and tell an adult.
fact: You can show affection your own way, such as a high-five or a wave instead of a kiss.
fact: You choose what is yours to share, but you can say no kindly.
fact: People online may not be who they say they are, so never send them photos or personal information.
fact: Cigarettes, vapes and alcohol harm a growing body.
exercise: Max breaks a vase by accident while playing. What should he do? | Tell an adult straight away and say sorry | Hide all the pieces under the sofa and say nothing about it | Say the cat must have knocked it over | Glue it back and hope nobody notices
exercise: Ruby does not want to try out for the play in case she gets it wrong. What is the best advice? | Try anyway, because you learn more by trying | Wait until she is sure she will be perfect | Only try things that she already knows | Let someone else try out instead of her, so she cannot get anything wrong
exercise: Leo gets a bad mark on a spelling test. What should he do? | Look at what he missed and ask the teacher for help | Decide he is bad at spelling forever | Hide the test from his parents so that nobody at home ever finds out | Tell everyone the test was unfair
exercise: You made a mistake and are tempted to blame your brother. What should you do? | Own up to it yourself | Blame him, because he is younger | Say nothing and let him be told off | Say you both did it
exercise: Why is blaming others for your mistakes a bad idea? | It is unfair and people stop trusting you | You always get caught straight away | It makes your mistake bigger | Other people will always blame you back, even when it was not you
exercise: A classmate spills paint all over her picture. What should you do? | Be kind and do not laugh | Laugh so she sees it is funny | Tell her the picture was bad anyway | Tell the teacher she was careless
exercise: You did not understand what the teacher just explained. What should you do? | Put up your hand and ask, or ask after class | Pretend to understand now and just guess the answers later on | Copy your neighbour's work instead | Wait and hope it is not on the test
exercise: A girl in Ireland wants to talk to someone in private about a problem. Who can she call? | Childline on 1800 66 66 66, which is free and private | Any phone number she happens to find on a website | A stranger she met online | Nobody, because problems are private
exercise: A friend dares you to climb onto a high wall. What should you do? | Say no clearly and walk away | Climb a little way to look brave | Do it, but very carefully | Dare him to go first
exercise: Someone hugs you and you do not want it. What can you do? | Say no or stop, and tell an adult | Put up with it so you are polite | Say nothing and hope it ends quickly | Hug them back so it is not rude
exercise: A relative wants a kiss and you do not want to. What could you do? | Offer a high-five or a wave instead | Kiss them anyway because they are family | Run away and hide in your room | Tell them they are horrible
exercise: Someone keeps pressuring you to give them your new pens. What is right? | You can kindly say no to some things | You must share everything you own | You should give them all your pens | You should hide all your things at school
exercise: Someone online asks for a photo of you and your address. What should you do? | Say no, do not reply and tell a parent | Send a photo but not the address | Ask them for a photo of themselves first | Reply only if they seem friendly
exercise: An older child offers you a vape. What should you do? | Say no thanks and walk away | Try once so you know what it is like | Take it and throw it away later | Keep it to look cool in front of others
exercise: Why should children say no to cigarettes, vapes and alcohol? | They harm a growing body | They are only bad for adults | They are fine if you only try once | They only harm people who are ill
test: A girl in Vancouver scratches a neighbour's car with her bike. Nobody saw. What should she do? | Tell her parents and the neighbour, and say sorry | Ride away quickly before anyone notices | Wait and see whether the neighbour ever finds the scratch | Say a different child did it
test: Which shows the healthiest attitude to a bad test mark? | It shows me what to practise next | It proves I am not clever | It means the teacher does not like me | It means I should stop trying
test: A boy in Texas needs to talk to someone in private but cannot tell his family. Which number can he call? | 988 | 999 | 000 | 111
test: Why is it worth asking a question in class even if you think it is silly? | The teacher wants to help, and others may wonder too | Teachers give prizes to the children who ask the most questions | It stops the lesson for everyone | You should not ask, because it shows you are slow
test: Your friends call you a chicken for not jumping off the bridge into the river. What is true? | Your safety matters more than looking brave | You should jump so they stop teasing you | You should jump but only feet first | Being called a chicken is worse than getting hurt
test: Aunt Sue always wants a big hug, and you feel uncomfortable. Which is true? | You can choose a high-five or a wave instead | You must hug her every time, because she is family and it is rude not to | You must not tell anyone how you feel | You should stay away from her for good
test: A gamer you met online says he is ten and asks for your school name. What is wise? | Do not tell him, and tell a parent | Tell him, because he is also a child | Tell him if he tells you his school first | Give him a fake name of a nearby school
test: Your sister broke your mum's mug and you laugh at her. What would be kinder? | Be kind, because you would want the same | Laugh more so she sees it is no big deal | Tell your mum as fast as you can | Tell her she always breaks things
test: A classmate keeps asking for your snack every day. What is fair? | You can kindly say no, because you choose what to share | You must always give it, because sharing is good | You should hide your snack at the bottom of your bag every day | You should tell the whole class that he begs
test: smashed mums vase playing ball, nobody saw 😬 | Tell an adult straight away and say sorry | Hide the pieces under the sofa n hope nobody looks | Say the cat knocked it over | Glue it and say nothing
test: got 3 out of 20 on the spelling, im just thick | No, look at what you missed and ask for help | Yes, one bad mark shows who you really are | Yes, so stop trying spelling | Yes, some people cannot learn
test: in Canada, feel awful, cant tell anyone at home, who can i call | Kids Help Phone, 1-800-668-6868 | Childline, 0800 1111 | Kids Helpline, 1800 55 1800 | Youthline, 0800 376 633
test: aunty wants a kiss but i dont wanna | You can say no and give a wave or high-five | You have to kiss family | Kiss her, or she will be sad all day | Run away and hide from her
explain: A girl in California said yes when an older kid asked her to keep a secret about touching; she felt worse and worse until she told her mum, who got help and kept her safe.
explain: A boy in Leeds broke a neighbour's window with a ball and hid; when it came out he was in more trouble for hiding than for the window.
fact: A secret that makes you feel scared, confused or uncomfortable is one you should tell a trusted adult.
fact: Owning up to a mistake quickly usually leads to less trouble than hiding it.
fact: If you get lost in a shop, ask a shop worker or a parent with children for help, and never go off with someone who offers you a lift.
test: older kid says keep a secret about touching, or else | Say no and tell a trusted adult, that's never a secret to keep | Keep it, you promised, and promises are promises | Only tell your friend | Wait to see if it happens again
test: smashed the neighbour's window with my ball, gonna hide | Own up and say sorry, hiding makes it worse | Hide and blame the wind | Run away until dark | Buy a new ball and forget it, nobody saw anyway
test: lost, can't find mum in the shop, too shy to ask anyone | Ask a shop worker or a parent with kids for help | Leave and walk home alone | Hide until closing time | Go with anyone at all who offers to give you a lift home
test: did u hear, Tom's mate jumped off the garage roof for a dare n broke both legs, they say he'll never walk right again. what's the real lesson | Say no to dangerous dares, because safety matters more than looking brave | Dares like that are fine if you are older and stronger than the others in the group | Only jump if your friends are watching to help | Jumping is safe as long as you land on grass
test: girl in year 6 apparently sent a photo to some "13 yr old" online n it was really a 50 yr old man, now the police are everywhere, my sister said. what's the bit to believe | People online may not be who they say, so never send them photos | All of it, every single detail exactly as your sister said it happened | None of it, people online are always who they say | Only that the police come whenever photos are sent
test: Jamie smashed the neighbour's window n hid in the shed lol, everyone's laughing, wanna tell the whole class? | No, do not spread it; the lesson is that owning up beats hiding | Yes, because it is funny and everyone at school should know what he did | Yes, but only after adding a few extra details | Yes, so the neighbour finds out who did it
exam: Identify the principal reason for which an accidental breakage ought to be reported to an adult without delay. | Prompt admission usually occasions less trouble than concealment | Prompt admission transfers responsibility to another person | Concealment is permissible provided that no adult witnessed the event | Adults are required to overlook any immediate admission
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a secret that causes a child fear or discomfort? | It ought to be disclosed to a trusted adult | It ought to be kept because a promise was given | It ought to be disclosed only to younger children | It ought to be forgotten as quickly as possible
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning cigarettes, vapes and alcohol in respect of children. | They cause harm to a body which is still growing | They are harmless when consumed in small amounts | Vapes alone are harmless to a growing body | They are harmful solely to persons of advanced age
exam: Whereas a person encountered online requests photographs and personal information, determine the appropriate response. | Decline, since such persons may not be who they claim | Comply, provided the person states his age as twelve | Comply, provided the photographs show no face | Decline only if the request has been made on more than one occasion
